WEBVTT


00:00:05.356 --> 00:00:08.356
Hello, welcome back to our course. Today, we are going to talk about reading and writing data.

00:00:08.807 --> 00:00:17.807
There are many sources of data. We could think about data in human-readable format, like CSV or TXT,

00:00:17.403 --> 00:00:27.403
data in binary format, like Excel, SPSS, STATA, or data from relational databases like Oracle and SAP.

00:00:32.056 --> 00:00:36.056
And R has more than 100 built-in datasets, which can be displayed by executing

00:00:41.034 --> 00:00:44.034
ls("package:datasets"). So here you can see very first few

00:00:44.107 --> 00:00:51.107
datasets in R, and actually many packages provide their own datasets.

00:00:59.822 --> 00:01:04.822
Data() shows all datasets of loaded packages. So here, of course, an example. If we, for example, call data(iris),

00:01:06.744 --> 00:01:08.744
then this loads dataset IRIS.

00:01:08.637 --> 00:01:14.637
And then here, if we'd like to see how these data look like,

00:01:15.105 --> 00:01:20.105
we could basically apply a function head() to iris, and then we can just see the first

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:26.540
4 rows. So you can see that this is a dataset with different species of irises.

00:01:29.569 --> 00:01:34.569
And these species actually differ by sepal and petal

00:01:43.898 --> 00:01:45.898
lengths, and widths. And, as you can see, a class of this dataset is a data frame, so you can see that data frames are indeed very useful.

00:01:53.639 --> 00:01:55.639
How can we handle external data not data already included in R, but external data? Well,

00:01:56.134 --> 00:02:02.134
we have two main functions for doing that, read.table() and write.table(),

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.280
a very general function and for all kinds of text-based data.

00:02:11.645 --> 00:02:14.645
And we also have read.csv, and write.csv, this is for CSV files.

00:02:14.877 --> 00:02:24.877
And then there are also specific functions for reading data from Excel, SQL, and they are available through specific packages.

00:02:26.027 --> 00:02:32.027
So let us study in detail, the syntax of the function read.table.

00:02:31.996 --> 00:02:37.996
So first, we need to select the file. The file is the name of the file to be read.

00:02:45.642 --> 00:02:50.642
Then, we have this parameter header. It's a logical value indicating whether the file contains the names of the variables or not.

00:02:50.616 --> 00:02:55.616
sep is the field separator character, so basically we tell R

00:03:03.584 --> 00:03:06.584
what was the character for separating different variables. Was it a tabulated file? Was it a comma, semicolon, space, etc?

00:03:09.779 --> 00:03:15.779
That stands for the decimal point used for, basically, reading the digits of a number.

00:03:19.322 --> 00:03:23.322
And row.names is a vector of row names. And similarly, the syntax is very similar for read.csv.

00:03:33.531 --> 00:03:37.531
When it comes to write.table, the syntax is similar, but before even file, we have this X. X is the object to be written. Imagine that we are programming, and then we

00:03:38.081 --> 00:03:42.081
had a data frame we wanted to save, that would be that X.

00:03:42.062 --> 00:03:47.062
Then the file is once again a character string naming the file

00:03:53.682 --> 00:03:55.682
including the file extension. Separator string is, here denoted by SEP,

00:03:55.987 --> 00:04:00.987
Na is the string to use for missing values in our data.

00:04:00.248 --> 00:04:06.248
Dec is once again a character to use in the file for decimal points.

00:04:06.917 --> 00:04:12.917
And row.names, it's a logical variable. Default is TRUE, so if we don't want to have our

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:20.150
rows named one two, I don't know, the number of the rows, we just indicate row.names

00:04:23.819 --> 00:04:29.819
FALSE. And similar syntax is valid for write.csv. How can we access

00:04:29.381 --> 00:04:36.381
folders, for example, to then write a specific directory for our file?

00:04:36.482 --> 00:04:43.482
Per default, R accesses your working directory to read or write data.

00:04:48.443 --> 00:04:51.443
If you'd like to access a file in a subfolder, you can just write file, and then in this quotation mark

00:04:51.518 --> 00:04:56.518
.\ the name of this subfolder, and the name of the file.

00:04:56.728 --> 00:05:00.728
If you would like to access a file in the parent directory,

00:05:05.036 --> 00:05:07.036
then, we have this ..\ and my file.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.320
We could, of course, type the entire file path

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:22.720
if our file is not either in this subfolder or in the parent folder.

00:05:23.302 --> 00:05:31.302
And now here we have an example, so here we would like to read a table, we would like to read a file called iris.txt.

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:37.033
The header of this file is TRUE, meaning that we have the names of the variables.

00:05:39.681 --> 00:05:43.681
Separator is a space, decimal point is dot. And row names, we don't have them, so NULL.

00:05:45.661 --> 00:05:48.661
The class of the iris is data.frame. And we could see that, indeed,

00:05:51.787 --> 00:05:54.787
we have, a data frame with different variables

00:06:03.715 --> 00:06:07.715
and different rows as observations. The dimension of our dataset can be checked applying function DIM to the iris, so we can see that it's 150 rows

00:06:07.286 --> 00:06:15.286
and 5 columns. And now, if I would like to create a new dataset, for example, I would like to create a

00:06:22.698 --> 00:06:24.698
subset of my dataset, we could, apply bracketing here. So, I would like to now create a subset

00:06:29.666 --> 00:06:33.666
of data, in which, sepal.length is larger equal to 3. How do I do it? Well, I

00:06:36.102 --> 00:06:40.102
open the bracket next to the name of my dataset. And then I select

00:06:39.754 --> 00:06:46.754
those rows for which sepal.width was larger equal than 3, and this is on the first place of my

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:53.370
first bracket. And then we leave the second place in the bracket empty.

00:06:54.042 --> 00:06:57.042
And now we can see that indeed the dimension of my new

00:07:00.242 --> 00:07:02.242
dataset is smaller. And now, if I would like to write this

00:07:05.497 --> 00:07:08.497
table, this dataset, to any file, how do I proceed? I first select

00:07:08.601 --> 00:07:13.601
what I like to write, so this is our object, this is our data frame, so this is new.diris

00:07:17.499 --> 00:07:19.499
Then I select the name of the file

00:07:25.542 --> 00:07:30.542
and iris.txt, then a separator, that would be a space. How do I handle missing data? So we call missing data NAs.

00:07:31.015 --> 00:07:36.015
Decimal point is a dot, and row names is FALSE, because I wouldn't like to have any

00:07:45.995 --> 00:07:50.995
row names in my new data set. And this is a summary function. Summary function is useful for checking some summary statistics

00:07:50.905 --> 00:07:56.905
for variables in our dataset, so you can see that if I apply this function to our dataset,

00:08:03.342 --> 00:08:07.342
I have some general summary statistics for each variable, I have minimum, I have two quartiles, I have median, I have mean,

00:08:15.194 --> 00:08:17.194
and I have maximum. And for the last column, you can see that this is

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:21.400
not a numeric variable, so we can see that we have length,

00:08:26.228 --> 00:08:28.228
we have a class, and we have a mode of this particular variable.

00:08:28.958 --> 00:08:32.958
And when it comes to reading files in R from other sources,

00:08:32.682 --> 00:08:38.682
there are specific packages for that. For example, for reading Excel files, we have a package, xlsx

00:08:38.257 --> 00:08:45.257
or read_excel(). For SPSS files, we can select a

00:08:50.654 --> 00:08:52.654
function from package foreign. For SAS files, we have a sas7bdat file package,

00:09:00.042 --> 00:09:07.042
and for EViews, we have another package hexView, etc. So, you can see that R is very flexible when it comes to reading data from various sources.

00:09:06.495 --> 00:09:11.495
Okay, that's it for today. Thank you, and see you in the next video.

